{
  "version": "1.0",
  "title": "LLaVA architecture: visual-to-language projection",
  "subtitle": "Paper-grounded architecture-only interpretation of original LLaVA.",
  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "paper-summary",
      "kind": "paper",
      "locator": "https://proceedings.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/6dcf277ea32ce3288914faf369fe6de0-Paper-Conference.pdf#page=4",
      "text": "# Paper-grounded design brief: original LLaVA\n\nSource: [Visual Instruction Tuning, NeurIPS 2023](https://proceedings.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/file/6dcf277ea32ce3288914faf369fe6de0-Paper-Conference.pdf), Sections 4.1–4.2, Figure 1 and Equation 1.\nThis is an authored summary for a new example, not a revision of the original\nsix-sentence illustrative VLM source. No paper artwork is copied or redistributed.\n\nAn image passes through the CLIP ViT-L/14 visual encoder. A learned linear matrix\nW maps its features Z_v into language-space visual embeddings H_v, with\nH_v = W Z_v. These visual embeddings and the instruction's text embeddings condition\nVicuna, which generates a language response autoregressively.\n\nThe training schedule carries information absent from the earlier generic example:\nalignment trains only W while retaining both backbones; subsequent instruction\ntuning updates W and Vicuna while the vision encoder stays frozen.\n\nKeep the model's internals abstract and compact. Do not import a Q-Former, MLP\nprojector, cross-attention adapter, exact token/layer counts or performance claims.\nDo not label all of Vicuna as frozen without distinguishing the training stage.\nNo figure title, footer or explanatory fine print is requested.\n"
    }
  ],
  "claims": {
    "summary": "A learned linear projection connects CLIP image features to Vicuna language-space conditioning.",
    "innovation": "The figure highlights the lightweight vision-to-language interface; it does not claim linear layers are themselves novel.",
    "baseline": "Pretrained CLIP visual encoder and Vicuna language model.",
    "scope": "Architecture only, single-turn conditioning abstraction. Training stages remain in METHOD.md; no decoder internals, fixed counts, or performance claims are drawn."
  },
  "assumptions": [
    "Evidence quotes in this specification are from the explicitly authored, source-backed METHOD.md summary, not verbatim quotations from the paper. The primary paper sections were independently inspected.",
    "Token motifs and frozen notation are symbolic; their repeated glyph counts do not state dimensions or exact model depth.",
    "The cat image and example instruction are illustrative inputs, not a reported paper evaluation sample."
  ],
